<PageLayout title="Editorial standards" eyebrow="Our newsroom" dek="How Arahkaii reports, verifies, corrects and labels its work." description="How Arahkaii reports, verifies, corrects and labels its work.">
		<p>Arahkaii publishes modern Asian luxury journalism with an emphasis on considered reporting and trustworthy recommendations. These standards explain how the work is commissioned, checked, labelled and corrected.</p>
		<section><h2>Reporting and sourcing</h2><p>Writers identify the people, places, products and evidence behind material claims. Health, finance, sustainability and certification claims require a named primary or authoritative source. Anonymous sourcing is exceptional and must be approved by an editor.</p></section>
		<section><h2>Reviews and recommendations</h2><p>Recommendations are based on reporting, direct experience or clearly attributed research. Guides state material limitations, prices and verification dates where available. Inclusion is never guaranteed by access, gifting or advertising.</p></section>
		<section><h2>How we verify dining recommendations</h2><p>Our dining and travel recommendations are checked per venue, and our editorial position is that we do not cover alcohol or nightlife. Where a recommendation depends on a time-sensitive detail — sourcing, certification or a kitchen's practices — we note the date checked and, where relevant, the source. Readers should confirm time-sensitive details with the venue directly before booking.</p></section>
		<section><h2>Corrections and updates</h2><p>Material corrections are made promptly and identified on the article with an updated date or correction note. Minor spelling and formatting changes may be made without a note. Send a correction with the article URL and supporting evidence through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.</p></section>
		<section><h2>Independence, advertising and affiliates</h2><p>Commercial work is labelled. Sponsored content, supplied access and affiliate relationships do not remove the obligation to report accurately. Editorial staff retain final control over conclusions, wording and publication.</p></section>
		<section><h2>AI and synthetic media</h2><p>AI may support research organisation, transcription, production tooling or clearly credited image creation. It does not replace editorial accountability. A named editor reviews published work, and synthetic images must not be presented as documentary evidence.</p></section>
		<section><h2>Images and rights</h2><p>Images require a credit and a recorded rights decision. Official or licensed imagery is preferred for named people and places. Generated images are credited as such and checked for misleading detail.</p></section>
		<section><h2>Last reviewed</h2><p>12 July 2026. These standards are reviewed when the publication changes its editorial processes or commercial model.</p></section>
</PageLayout>
